<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raj Gond</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> Gonds are the ruling and landowning section of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gond-people">Gond people</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Gond kingdoms such as Garha-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandla">Mandla</a>, Deogarh and Chanda dominated the region from the 15th to the 18th century, and Raj Gond families held several smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a>, including Sarangarh, Raigarh, Makrai and Kawardha, into the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raj-gond.jpg" alt="Raj Gond" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yann (talk) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Women_in_adivasi_village,_Umaria_district,_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
